WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.100
Your mornings are short, and
your ledger explains every line.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.833
Now the road a change takes
before it reaches that morning.

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:08.367
Change anything safely, then
promote it with confidence.

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:11.333
By the end you'll know where a
change lands, what's real there,

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:13.400
and how it reaches
production with one approval.

00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:16.033
Leo edits the scoring model
in the last week of a quarter.

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:19.067
By the next pipeline review, the
Hot list has reshuffled under Mia.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.367
Nobody read the new ranking first.
Here, that change waits in dev.

00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:24.600
Every organization has three environments.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.667
Dev is where every change lands
first: blueprints you apply,

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:30.667
definitions you edit, and the
plays and apps Studio ships.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.033
Staging is where you rehearse.

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:35.900
Prod is what your organization actually
runs: the runtime, scheduled plays,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.667
bindings and the research
agent all read prod.

00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:40.833
Now the rule behind most surprises.

00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:42.533
An engine uses the signed version of a

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:44.600
definition in the
environment the call runs in,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.133
and otherwise its built in default.

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.600
A draft, or one that's
only proposed, is ignored.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.500
So when a change seems to do nothing,
it usually lives in dev, is unsigned,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.000
or the call ran somewhere else.

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:57.033
The switcher in the header shows which
environment your console is reading.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:00.267
The gotcha is that a change set runs
in the environment it was proposed for,

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:01.433
whatever environment you're viewing.

00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:04.400
Approving a dev change while you
look at prod doesn't push it to prod.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.133
Or ask your agent:

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.667
"List the environments and tell
me which one you are reading".

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.933
Now what's real in each. Config
changes apply in their own environment,

00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:13.533
so a definition you apply
to dev is live in dev.

00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:14.933
Customer facing effects differ.

00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:18.867
In dev and staging, a send, a CRM
write or a publish is recorded,

00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:21.533
not performed. The approval
reads not applied, dev.

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:23.000
And only prod fires for real.

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:26.433
Step one, apply to dev, so every
change starts where it can't hurt.

00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:29.567
In the console: Environments,
or a blueprint's Apply button.

00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:33.200
On the command line, gtm plan previews,
and gtm apply with env dev applies.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:33.833
Or ask your agent:

00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:36.533
"Apply the outbound blueprint
to dev and show me the plan".

00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:40.233
It becomes a change set, and a different
person approves it in Approvals.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:44.000
You can't apply a definition straight
to prod, because the system refuses.

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:45.233
Step two, validate in dev.

00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:48.333
Run the engine, then read what
it did in the actions ledger,

00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:51.300
and because effects are recorded,
not sent, you can run it freely.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.233
Mia checks the ranking
against her sense of fit.

00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:56.367
In the console: switch to dev in
the header, then Engines, Score.

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:59.333
Or ask your agent to score an
account in dev and explain it.

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:02.700
Step three, sign, because engines
read only signed definitions.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.167
Your definitions, signed once.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:07.167
Propose the definitions for signing,
and a person who isn't the proposer,

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:10.067
or the human behind a
proposing agent, signs them.

00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:12.833
In the console: Environments, Go
live, Review and sign all proposed.

00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:13.633
On the command line,

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.400
gtm definition sign all with env
dev signs everything in one go.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.367
Or ask your agent: "Which definitions
are waiting for a signature".

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.833
Step four, promote. The Environments
page shows drift as a banner,

00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:26.400
naming the definitions that
are in dev and not live.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.900
Open Go live for the plan
diff and an impact preview,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.400
then choose Promote dev to prod.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.767
It's one approval, and the change walks
hop by hop, dev to staging to prod,

00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:36.267
re-checking each item on the way.

00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:39.033
Anything that changed after
approval is skipped, not guessed.

00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:41.667
On the command line, gtm
promote all dev prod does it.

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:44.933
Or ask your agent: "Preview promoting
everything from dev to prod".

00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:48.133
Operator tip: add preview first,
every time, to see the plan.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.900
Read the plan before you approve it.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.367
Each resource lists its
version and a top level diff.

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.733
Some are skipped, each with its reason:

00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:57.533
an unsigned definition bound
for prod, a deprecated version,

00:02:57.533 --> 00:03:00.500
a per environment package default,
or something already live.

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.800
Step five, roll back when
a change disappoints.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.467
It's a proposal like any other,
a different person approves,

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:07.033
and only that definition changes.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:10.267
In the console: Environments, pick
the definition and an earlier version.

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:12.167
On the command line, gtm
rollback takes the kind,

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:14.533
the key and the version to return to.

00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:17.433
Or ask your agent: "Roll back the
scoring model to the previous version".

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:20.900
To catch drift before it bites,
gtm check compares environments,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.333
in a script or in CI. The Environments
banner shows the same thing.

00:03:24.333 --> 00:03:27.133
Or ask your agent: "What
is in dev and not live".

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:30.033
Need another environment? Create a
named one, for a client rehearsal,

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:33.500
as a proposal that needs an approval.
Studio ships into dev by default.

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:34.667
Three messages you'll meet.

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:37.300
When it says the source version
is draft, sign it first.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.133
When it says you proposed this
version, a different person signs.

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:44.033
And an illegal promotion from prod to
dev means promotion only moves upward.

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:45.500
A typical week. On Monday,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.300
Leo changes the scoring model in dev and

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.167
rehearses it in staging with Dry Mode on,

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:50.600
while Sophia reads the drafts.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.367
On Tuesday the definitions are
signed, one approval promotes them,

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:56.533
and Mia checks the Hot list. Nothing
touched production until Tuesday.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.467
The pushback: three environments
is process for its own sake.

00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:01.900
The system enforces it, so
nobody has to remember it.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.867
You can't apply straight to prod.
You can't sign your own proposal.

00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:06.567
And promotion only moves upward.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.367
The short version: Change in
dev, a different person signs,

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:12.433
and one approval promotes it. Roll
back one definition when you need to.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.767
So you change anything
without touching production,

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:17.600
and you can say exactly when it went live.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.600
Run gtm check today and see what's in dev.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.833
After this comes Tutorial: going
live, your first real send, safely.
